Docker:  					
=======
Docker is an open-source containerization platform that allows you to package an application along with all its dependencies (libraries, runtime, configuration files, etc.) into a container.

A container can run consistently on any system that has Docker installed.

Why Docker Was Created
=======================

Before Docker, applications often worked on one machine but failed on another because of differences in:

	Operating System
	Library versions
	Runtime versions
	Environment variables
	
Example:

Developer Laptop
     |
     | Works Fine
     v
Production Server
     |
     | Error: Java/Python/Node version mismatch
     v
Application Fails



Note:   Docker solves this by packaging everything together.
===================================================

What is a Container?

A container is a lightweight, isolated environment that contains:

	Application code
	Runtime
	Libraries
	Dependencies
	Configuration files


+----------------------+
| Docker Container     |
|----------------------|
| Application          |
| Libraries            |
| Dependencies         |
| Runtime              |
+----------------------+


Docker vs Virtual Machine
==========================
1) Virtual Machine (VM)
-----------------------

Physical Server
      |
 Hypervisor
   /      \
 VM1      VM2
(OS)      (OS)


Each VM has its own operating system.

Disadvantages:

	Heavy
	Uses more RAM
	Slow startup
	
	
2)  Docker Containers
-----------------------

Physical Server
      |
 Host OS
      |
 Docker Engine
   /    |    \
 C1    C2    C3
 
 
 Containers share the host OS kernel.
 
 
 Advantages:

		Lightweight
		Fast startup
		Less memory usage
		Easy deployment
		
